{"prompt": "My flight got cancelled and I have a meeting in Berlin tomorrow morning. What do I do?", "response": "Let's fix this step by step. First, ask the airline to rebook you — under EU261 they must offer re-routing at the earliest opportunity, including on other carriers. While you're in line, check tonight's trains: the overnight NJ 40490 gets you to Berlin Hbf by 08:14. If neither works, message your meeting organizer now with two options: join the first hour remotely, or push to the afternoon. Want me to draft that message?"}
{"prompt": "How do I stop my sourdough from going flat when I bake it?", "response": "Flat loaves almost always mean under-strength dough or over-proofing. Three things to try, in order: (1) do 3-4 stretch-and-folds in the first two hours; (2) proof in the fridge overnight and bake straight from cold; (3) check your starter doubles within 6 hours of feeding — if not, feed it twice daily for a few days before baking again."}
{"prompt": "I keep getting a segfault in my C program but the debugger only shows it crashing after the problematic line. How do I track this down?", "response": "Classic delayed-segv scenario. The crash happens after the bad access because memory corruption often manifests later when that corrupted memory is dereferenced. Try these in order: (1) run under Valgrind: `valgrind --tool=memcheck ./yourprog` — it will flag the exact allocation/access that goes wrong; (2) if you need speed, use AddressSanitizer by recompiling with `gcc -fsanitize=address -g` — it instruments your code and catches the error at the source line; (3) check for off-by-one errors in array loops, use-after-free, and missing null terminators on strings, which are the usual suspects."}
{"prompt": "I just got diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and the doctor said to change my diet but didn't give specifics. Where do I start without feeling overwhelmed?", "response": "Start with the one change that has the biggest impact: reduce refined carbs. Here's a concrete first week: (1) Replace white bread/rice/pasta with whole grain versions, or swap to legumes/quinoa; (2) Always pair carbs with protein or fat — e.g., an apple with almonds instead of alone, which slows glucose absorption; (3) Check your meals using the plate method: half vegetables, quarter lean protein, quarter complex carbs. Don't try to eliminate carbs entirely — that's unsustainable. Recheck your fasting glucose in 2 weeks and share the trend with your doctor."}
{"prompt": "I'm moving to Tokyo for work in 3 months. What's the absolute minimum I need to sort out before arrival?", "response": "Three priorities, in order: (1) Housing — secure a place for at least your first month. Use a temporary apartment service like Oakhouse or a capsule hotel chain while you house-hunt in person; Japanese landlords often require a guarantor you won't have yet. (2) Work visa — if your company hasn't already sponsored your Certificate of Eligibility, escalate this now; processing takes 1–3 months. (3) Phone — buy a Japanese SIM on arrival at Narita/Haneda kiosks; eSIM options like Ubigi work for the first few days. Everything else (bank account, health insurance, address registration) can be handled within your first week with your residence card."}
{"prompt": "My 8-year-old is struggling with reading comprehension. They can decode words fine but don't remember or understand what they read. Any practical strategies?", "response": "This is more common than you think — word calling without comprehension. Try these evidence-based techniques: (1) Read aloud together and pause after each paragraph to ask \"What just happened?\" in their own words — this builds active listening; (2) Use think-aloud modelling: read a paragraph and verbalise your thinking (\"Hmm, the author says she's anxious because...\"); (3) Teach the SQ3R method simplified: Survey headings first, Question what they expect, Read, Recite the main point, Review; (4) Start with shorter, high-interest texts at their level — magazines, comics, or chapter books with illustrations. If this persists beyond 6–8 weeks, ask their teacher about an evaluation for working memory or attention factors."}
{"prompt": "I need to write a resignation letter but I want to leave on good terms. It's a small team and I'll be working with these people after I leave. Help me draft something that's professional but warm.", "response": "Here's a framework that balances professionalism with genuine warmth — adjust the specifics to fit your situation:\n\n\"Dear [Manager's Name],\n\nI'm writing to let you know I've accepted a new opportunity and my last day will be [date, giving standard notice].\n\nThis wasn't an easy decision. I've genuinely valued my time here — [mention one specific thing: a project, mentorship, or the team dynamic]. Thank you for [specific thanks].\n\nOver the next [weeks], I'm committed to a smooth transition: documenting my workflows, wrapping up [specific task], and training [colleague] on my responsibilities. I'm happy to help with the handover however I can.\n\nI'd love to stay in touch. [Optional: personal contact info].\n\nBest regards,\n[Your name]\"\n\nKey tips: deliver this in person or on video call first, then send the letter. Keep it forward-looking — no criticism, even if you're leaving for negative reasons."}
